{"tool_name":"giniqc","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"giniqc","home":"https://github.com/4dn-dcic/GiniQC","summary":"A measure for quantifying noise in single-cell Hi-C data.\n\nSUMMARY:Single-cell Hi-C (scHi-C) allows the study of cell-to-cell variability in chromatin structure and dynamics. However, the high level of noise inherent in current scHi-C protocols necessitates careful assessment of data quality before biological conclusions can be drawn.","addition_date":"2021-01-18T07:31:33Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T20:47:38.707811Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Script"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:giniqc","credit":[{"email":"peter_park@hms.harvard.edu","name":"Peter J Park","typeEntity":"Person"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Standardisation and normalisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3435"},{"term":"Validation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2428"}]}],"name":"GiniQC","owner":"Niclaskn","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btaa048","metadata":{"abstract":"Single-cell Hi-C (scHi-C) allows the study of cell-to-cell variability in chromatin structure and dynamics. However, the high level of noise inherent in current scHi-C protocols necessitates careful assessment of data quality before biological conclusions can be drawn. Here, we present GiniQC, which quantifies unevenness in the distribution of inter-chromosomal reads in the scHi-C contact matrix to measure the level of noise. Our examples show the utility of GiniQC in assessing the quality of scHi-C data as a complement to existing quality control measures. We also demonstrate how GiniQC can help inform the impact of various data processing steps on data quality.","authors":[{"name":"Alver B.H."},{"name":"Horton C.A."},{"name":"Park P.J."}],"citationCount":3,"date":"2020-05-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics","title":"GiniQC: A measure for quantifying noise in single-cell Hi-